(2278662, 310071, 8860, '<td valign="top">Thanks Alex - very flattering that you chose this shot for your entry.<br/> <br/>I''ve got to say that it feels very strange seeing this - it''s almost like deja-vu. Oh, yes, that''s the point isn''t it.<br/> <br/>I think you''ve done a great job emulating the shot, and the high tide adds a different ''spot-the-difference'' kind of element to the photo.<br/> <br/>I wouldn''t be doing you justice if I didn''t commment critically, so:<br/>&gt; you''ve vastly improved the colour by making it more freshly blue - a change which I''ve added in the print of this by removing some cyan and yellow<br/>&gt; the detail in the dome is great too - richer and more detailed<br/>&gt; you''ve caught more detail in the office buildings on the left too<br/>&gt; the high tide has removed a bit of extra feature from the pillars - which I like so much and miss in this version<br/>&gt; you''ve over-dodged it a tad in the river, pillar and railing areas and I miss some of the reflections<br/>&gt; I''m puzzled by the difference in lighting on the bridge which made me wonder what time of evening this was shot<br/>&gt; I think I prefer a more portrait composition<br/> <br/>Thanks for the submission and I hope you take these comments in the friendly spirit they''re intended. I hope you gained something from the experience too. I find this kind of process very useful in the same way that performing a cover version in music can be an eye-opener - as I also found myself when entering the Ansel Adams challenge (and I''m not comparing myself here - perisjh the thought).<br/> <br/>All the best<br/>Jon</td>', 'Thanks Alex - very flattering that you chose this shot for your entry. I''ve got to say that it feels very strange seeing this - it''s almost like deja-vu. Oh, yes, that''s the point isn''t it. I think you''ve done a great job emulating the shot, and the high tide adds a different ''spot-the-difference'' kind of element to the photo. I wouldn''t be doing you justice if I didn''t commment critically, so:> you''ve vastly improved the colour by making it more freshly blue - a change which I''ve added in the print of this by removing some cyan and yellow> the detail in the dome is great too - richer and more detailed> you''ve caught more detail in the office buildings on the left too> the high tide has removed a bit of extra feature from the pillars - which I like so much and miss in this version> you''ve over-dodged it a tad in the river, pillar and railing areas and I miss some of the reflections> I''m puzzled by the difference in lighting on the bridge which made me wonder what time of evening this was shot> I think I prefer a more portrait composition Thanks for the submission and I hope you take these comments in the friendly spirit they''re intended. I hope you gained something from the experience too. I find this kind of process very useful in the same way that performing a cover version in music can be an eye-opener - as I also found myself when entering the Ansel Adams challenge (and I''m not comparing myself here - perisjh the thought). All the bestJon', '2006-04-01 16:43:03.000000', NULL, 0),
