(2291889, 307903, 47117, '<td valign="top">Very pretty. I gave this an 8.  Love the colors, the background, and the look. She is darling!</td>', 'Very pretty. I gave this an 8.  Love the colors, the background, and the look. She is darling!', '2006-04-06 11:46:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(2292181, 309486, 32321, '<td valign="top">Hi! Heres a comment from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>First Impression:<br/>Looks interesting! I recognize the déjà vu!<br/> <br/>Composition:<br/>Good location for the hat, legs provide nice leading lines. Background nice contrast for the legs. I like the colour and the colour fade of the background. Left foot looks a little stiff. I dont wear high heals, so I dont know if you could have pointed the left toe a little more.<br/> <br/>Subject:<br/>Nice adaptation on the original. Meets challenge.<br/> <br/>Technical:<br/>Unfortunately you didnt provide any post processing information, so Im not sure what you were trying to achieve. I find the picture grainy. A second light, or bounced light could have given more detail to the Hat. The hat &amp; foot create a black hole. The legs almost appear to be over exposed. Some dodging and burning may have helped tone down the legs and bring out more detail in the hat.<br/> <br/>Summary:<br/>I liked the concept, but this was a really tough challenge. The pictures needed to really pop technically to do well. Its frustrating when a decent scoring picture places so low. <br/></td>', 'Hi! Heres a comment from the Critique Club. First Impression:Looks interesting! I recognize the déjà vu! Composition:Good location for the hat, legs provide nice leading lines. Background nice contrast for the legs. I like the colour and the colour fade of the background. Left foot looks a little stiff. I dont wear high heals, so I dont know if you could have pointed the left toe a little more. Subject:Nice adaptation on the original. Meets challenge. Technical:Unfortunately you didnt provide any post processing information, so Im not sure what you were trying to achieve. I find the picture grainy. A second light, or bounced light could have given more detail to the Hat. The hat & foot create a black hole. The legs almost appear to be over exposed. Some dodging and burning may have helped tone down the legs and bring out more detail in the hat. Summary:I liked the concept, but this was a really tough challenge. The pictures needed to really pop technically to do well. Its frustrating when a decent scoring picture places so low. ', '2006-04-06 14:29:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(2292210, 310577, 52336, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/>When first confronted with this photo, the impact is high, almost visceral, which is always very good.  The ripped pieces of paper along top and bottom are an excellent border for such a soft subject, with the brilliant white and torn edges a wonderful contrast to the velvety skin and "rougher" texture of brows and irises.  Lighting is excellent, with the catch-light in the eyes a lovely addition.  The position of the subject somewhat at the left edge of the frame, whether intentional or as a result of your desire to show the bent-back piece of paper, makes for a very satisfying composition, nicely offsetting the more formalized vertical placement of the subject and foreground.<br/> <br/>The model''s skin is slightly too post-processed, in my opinion.  I believe I can see actual brush strokes where you may have applied smudging or some equivalent brush technique, though this may also be the result of burning an area having uneven tone.  In any case, the post-processing is in my view evident, which usually detracts from a photo.  <br/> <br/>The shadow of the bent-back piece of paper is harsh, cutting off that corner of the image and drawing the eye there, which is not desired.  It could be argued, however, that this shadow balances or reflects the somewhat deeper shadow above it, to the right of the model, but for me, it traps the eye.<br/> <br/>In many portraits where the eyes are a pertinent feature, selectively sharpening the irises with care and restraint usually produces stunning results.  It may have made this photo very dramatic.<br/> <br/>Overall, an excellent photo; well done!<br/> <br/>Hope this helps,<br/>Louis</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  When first confronted with this photo, the impact is high, almost visceral, which is always very good.  The ripped pieces of paper along top and bottom are an excellent border for such a soft subject, with the brilliant white and torn edges a wonderful contrast to the velvety skin and "rougher" texture of brows and irises.  Lighting is excellent, with the catch-light in the eyes a lovely addition.  The position of the subject somewhat at the left edge of the frame, whether intentional or as a result of your desire to show the bent-back piece of paper, makes for a very satisfying composition, nicely offsetting the more formalized vertical placement of the subject and foreground. The model''s skin is slightly too post-processed, in my opinion.  I believe I can see actual brush strokes where you may have applied smudging or some equivalent brush technique, though this may also be the result of burning an area having uneven tone.  In any case, the post-processing is in my view evident, which usually detracts from a photo.   The shadow of the bent-back piece of paper is harsh, cutting off that corner of the image and drawing the eye there, which is not desired.  It could be argued, however, that this shadow balances or reflects the somewhat deeper shadow above it, to the right of the model, but for me, it traps the eye. In many portraits where the eyes are a pertinent feature, selectively sharpening the irises with care and restraint usually produces stunning results.  It may have made this photo very dramatic. Overall, an excellent photo; well done! Hope this helps,Louis', '2006-04-06 14:40:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(2292544, 309130, 27133, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by richabhatia:</b></div><hr/><i> Nice picture. I have a question though. Is the background a natural landscape (sunrise) or otherwise? </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>No....the background is a light shining on a white board and with the WB on shade it gave it a nice orange hue.</td>', 'Originally posted by richabhatia: Nice picture. I have a question though. Is the background a natural landscape (sunrise) or otherwise?   No....the background is a light shining on a white board and with the WB on shade it gave it a nice orange hue.', '2006-04-06 18:26:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(2292698, 307903, 42013, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>OUch! First Joey Lawrence''s "shadow" ribbon winner, now a classic, ribbon winning, retake of a site favorite.  Here goes :)<br/> <br/>First impression: Beautiful picture, the child is beautiful, the colors and texture of her dress contrasting nicely with the rugged wall. Lovely picture.<br/> <br/>Technically I find your photo superb, with great skin tones, vibrant colors, sharpness, etc. etc.  All of the qualities which are the foundation of a great pic. The only nitpic I have is the shadow area surrounding her left eye. it would be nice to have just a little more clarity around her eye. Her hands are also natural, not classically posed, which is one more reason to love this pic.<br/> <br/>The feeling of your picture is also superb. The girl has such a pensive/thoughtful expression that it forces the viewer to wonder either what she is thinking or if something is wrong, giving the viewer a connection to the child.<br/> <br/>As it relates to the challenge...you took a very good ribbon winner and represented your own version very well. Perfect choice of photo and perfect treatment.<br/> <br/>Any questions?? Just PM me.  good luck in the future.<br/> <br/>Mark</td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club: OUch! First Joey Lawrence''s "shadow" ribbon winner, now a classic, ribbon winning, retake of a site favorite.  Here goes :) First impression: Beautiful picture, the child is beautiful, the colors and texture of her dress contrasting nicely with the rugged wall. Lovely picture. Technically I find your photo superb, with great skin tones, vibrant colors, sharpness, etc. etc.  All of the qualities which are the foundation of a great pic. The only nitpic I have is the shadow area surrounding her left eye. it would be nice to have just a little more clarity around her eye. Her hands are also natural, not classically posed, which is one more reason to love this pic. The feeling of your picture is also superb. The girl has such a pensive/thoughtful expression that it forces the viewer to wonder either what she is thinking or if something is wrong, giving the viewer a connection to the child. As it relates to the challenge...you took a very good ribbon winner and represented your own version very well. Perfect choice of photo and perfect treatment. Any questions?? Just PM me.  good luck in the future. Mark', '2006-04-06 19:49:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(2292722, 310633, 42013, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>Leroy, you had the chance to critique a landscape of mine, and did a fine job I might add, now its my turn :)<br/> <br/>First impression: Not visually appealing(to me) but technically good. I vaguely remember the picture you are payed homage to.<br/> <br/>Technically: As stated, good.  You know your stuff with lighting, focus, etc. etc.  Good job on the lighting gradient from lower left to upper right.  IN short(s), good technicals.<br/> <br/>Composition and subject matter: Im sure the diagonals created in this image were purposful. Water a good touch. Subject not as appealing to the general public as much as say...a flower, a child, or a solitary water drop :)<br/> <br/>Challenge: The picture is funny, probably the reaction you wanted judging by some of your various pictures scattered around DPC. Here is where I would take comfort in your score: it was a very high scoring picture to land on the back page of a challenge!  4.798 puts a person near the middle of the pack in many challenges.  So, take solace in that fact.<br/> <br/>anyway, keep it lively!<br/> <br/>mark<br/> <br/>PM me with any questions.</td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club Leroy, you had the chance to critique a landscape of mine, and did a fine job I might add, now its my turn :) First impression: Not visually appealing(to me) but technically good. I vaguely remember the picture you are payed homage to. Technically: As stated, good.  You know your stuff with lighting, focus, etc. etc.  Good job on the lighting gradient from lower left to upper right.  IN short(s), good technicals. Composition and subject matter: Im sure the diagonals created in this image were purposful. Water a good touch. Subject not as appealing to the general public as much as say...a flower, a child, or a solitary water drop :) Challenge: The picture is funny, probably the reaction you wanted judging by some of your various pictures scattered around DPC. Here is where I would take comfort in your score: it was a very high scoring picture to land on the back page of a challenge!  4.798 puts a person near the middle of the pack in many challenges.  So, take solace in that fact. anyway, keep it lively! mark PM me with any questions.', '2006-04-06 20:07:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(2292738, 309974, 42013, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>This is a nicely done picture, paying homage to Manny''s original.<br/> <br/>Composition: My first reaction is dislike of the reflection of the sky and the pencils in the mirror. It seems as if the multiple pencil reflections distract from the clean look of your copy when compared to the original. The blue sky alone with the pencils would have been OK IMO. The pencils are well placed, and rule of thirds composition is followed nicely. The colors are nice, as other stated.<br/> <br/>Technicals: The lighting seems harsh to my eyes. The addition of some diffusion material may have helped. Also, this may be my monitor, but there appears to be a reddish cast to the sharpened ends of each pencil. This tint detracts from the nice colors of the pencils and causes the colored points to be lost. I would have liked to see the natural wood of the pencils contrasted against the color of their tips. Possibly a little oversharpened in post processing IMO as there appears to be some fringing on some of the edge pixels.<br/> <br/>Challenge: Yours was a nice score in high-scoring challenge and it paid proper homage to the original.  Good job!<br/> <br/>If you have any questions please PM me.  Keep shooting!<br/> <br/>mark</td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club. This is a nicely done picture, paying homage to Manny''s original. Composition: My first reaction is dislike of the reflection of the sky and the pencils in the mirror. It seems as if the multiple pencil reflections distract from the clean look of your copy when compared to the original. The blue sky alone with the pencils would have been OK IMO. The pencils are well placed, and rule of thirds composition is followed nicely. The colors are nice, as other stated. Technicals: The lighting seems harsh to my eyes. The addition of some diffusion material may have helped. Also, this may be my monitor, but there appears to be a reddish cast to the sharpened ends of each pencil. This tint detracts from the nice colors of the pencils and causes the colored points to be lost. I would have liked to see the natural wood of the pencils contrasted against the color of their tips. Possibly a little oversharpened in post processing IMO as there appears to be some fringing on some of the edge pixels. Challenge: Yours was a nice score in high-scoring challenge and it paid proper homage to the original.  Good job! If you have any questions please PM me.  Keep shooting! mark', '2006-04-06 20:16:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(2292836, 309539, 42995, '<td valign="top">Just seeing this now, Jimmy -- great work!<br/></td>', 'Just seeing this now, Jimmy -- great work!', '2006-04-06 21:04:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(2292982, 309353, 52336, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/>Your photo is a well done tribute to the original, and an excellent photograph in its own right.  Regarding the features of your image specifically: overall, composition is wonderful, with the subject slightly off centre and weighted toward the bottom of the picture in a pleasing way.  Bokeh is perfect, and, needless to say, the colour is spectacular, truly one of the most pleasing elements.  I like your version''s slight emphasis on the bulb''s threads, which add interest, the highlights nicely offsetting the splash of colours behind.<br/> <br/>I understand it''s almost unavoidable at this exposure, but grain is noticeable in the colour background, which, for such an abstract composition, would not have suffered from aggressive noise reduction in NeatImage or similar.  However, it could be said that the grain enhances the effect.  There is lens debris or dust on the lightbulb (on the orange portion) that should have been cloned out.<br/> <br/>Minor points all, and your photo is not diminished by them.  A lovely image.  Congrats!<br/> <br/>I hope this has helped,<br/>Louis</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Your photo is a well done tribute to the original, and an excellent photograph in its own right.  Regarding the features of your image specifically: overall, composition is wonderful, with the subject slightly off centre and weighted toward the bottom of the picture in a pleasing way.  Bokeh is perfect, and, needless to say, the colour is spectacular, truly one of the most pleasing elements.  I like your version''s slight emphasis on the bulb''s threads, which add interest, the highlights nicely offsetting the splash of colours behind. I understand it''s almost unavoidable at this exposure, but grain is noticeable in the colour background, which, for such an abstract composition, would not have suffered from aggressive noise reduction in NeatImage or similar.  However, it could be said that the grain enhances the effect.  There is lens debris or dust on the lightbulb (on the orange portion) that should have been cloned out. Minor points all, and your photo is not diminished by them.  A lovely image.  Congrats! I hope this has helped,Louis', '2006-04-06 22:16:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(2293048, 309965, 52336, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/>On first view, this photograph took my breath away.  In my opinion, this is truly a spectacular land/seascape.  Composition is spot-on, the bench expertly reflecting lines in the near and far horizons.  The counter-lines offered by the incoming surf supply a lovely balance.  The slender plants coming off the edge of the greenery and extending into the view of the water are a perfect border, separating the two complementing views in this image with subtlety.  For me, the orientation of the bench offers an excellent invitation to study the rest of the picture, as it draws the eye gently into the drama of the background.  I think there is a certain balance achieved with the dark elements in the centre left of the image, that break up in an interesting way as they make their way across the image to the right.<br/> <br/>The clouds may have benefitted from subtle, restrained burning with a large soft brush to enhance the drama.  I''m loath to suggest this, though, since I really like this image as-is.<br/> <br/>A real feast for the eyes.  Thanks!<br/> <br/>Louis</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  On first view, this photograph took my breath away.  In my opinion, this is truly a spectacular land/seascape.  Composition is spot-on, the bench expertly reflecting lines in the near and far horizons.  The counter-lines offered by the incoming surf supply a lovely balance.  The slender plants coming off the edge of the greenery and extending into the view of the water are a perfect border, separating the two complementing views in this image with subtlety.  For me, the orientation of the bench offers an excellent invitation to study the rest of the picture, as it draws the eye gently into the drama of the background.  I think there is a certain balance achieved with the dark elements in the centre left of the image, that break up in an interesting way as they make their way across the image to the right. The clouds may have benefitted from subtle, restrained burning with a large soft brush to enhance the drama.  I''m loath to suggest this, though, since I really like this image as-is. A real feast for the eyes.  Thanks! Louis', '2006-04-06 22:47:32.000000', NULL, 0),
